---
// src/pages/antigravity/antimatter-gravity.astro

import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Does Antimatter Fall Up? Antigravity and Antimatter Gravity Experiments';
const description = 'Do antimatter and normal matter fall the same way? Explore antihydrogen experiments, gravitational measurements, and what current evidence suggests.';
const canonical = `${Astro.site}antigravity/antimatter-gravity/`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between antigravity and anti-matter?',
    a: 'Antimatter is real and consists of particles with opposite charges to normal matter. Antigravity is a hypothetical effect involving gravity. Antimatter does not currently provide evidence of antigravity.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does antimatter fall up or down in Earth gravity?',
    a: 'Antimatter falls down. In September 2023, the ALPHA-g collaboration at CERN published direct free-fall measurements demonstrating that magnetically released antihydrogen atoms fall downward under Earth gravity with an acceleration consistent with g = 9.81 m/s² (specifically ~0.75 ± 0.29 g, ruling out repulsive antigravity).',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the Weak Equivalence Principle (WEP)?',
    a: 'The Weak Equivalence Principle states that the gravitational acceleration of a test particle is independent of its composition and internal structure. The ALPHA-g and AEgIS experiments test whether antimatter obeys the exact same WEP as normal matter.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why was measuring antimatter gravity so difficult for decades?',
    a: 'Charged antiparticles (like positrons and antiprotons) are overwhelmingly affected by stray electric and magnetic fields. A stray field of just 1 volt/meter exerts a force on an antiproton 100 trillion times stronger than gravity. Only neutral antihydrogen atoms cooled to fractions of a Kelvin can isolate the tiny gravitational signal.',
  },
];

const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: title,
  description,
  mainEntityOfPage: canonical,
  author: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'NanoToMicro',
    url: Astro.site.toString(),
  },
};

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: f.q,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: f.a,
    },
  })),
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    {
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: 1,
      name: 'Home',
      item: Astro.site.toString(),
    },
    {
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: 2,
      name: 'Antigravity Cluster',
      item: `${Astro.site}antigravity/`,
    },
    {
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: 3,
      name: 'Antimatter Gravity',
      item: canonical,
    },
  ],
};

const jsonLd = [articleSchema, faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article>
    <div class="tag-row" style="margin-top: 0;">
      <a href="/antigravity/" class="tag">← Antigravity Hub</a>
      <span class="tag">Antimatter</span>
      <span class="tag">CERN Physics</span>
      <span class="tag">ALPHA-g</span>
      <span class="tag">General Relativity</span>
    </div>

    <h1>Does Antimatter Fall Up? Antigravity and Antimatter Gravity Experiments</h1>

    <p>
      For over a century, one of the most intriguing questions in fundamental physics was whether antimatter might experience <strong>repulsive gravity</strong> (falling upward in Earth's gravitational field). Such an effect would provide a direct mechanism for natural antigravity.
    </p>

    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">The Landmark 2023 CERN Finding</h3>
      <p class="muted">
        In September 2023, the ALPHA collaboration at CERN published in <em>Nature</em> direct observations confirming that <strong>antihydrogen atoms fall downward</strong> under Earth's gravity with an acceleration consistent with standard gravity (g ≈ 9.81 m/s²), definitively ruling out repulsive "antigravity" for antimatter.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>1. The CERN Antiproton Decelerator Experiments</h2>
      <p>
        Measuring gravity on antimatter required synthesizing electrically neutral atoms (antihydrogen, consisting of an antiproton orbited by a positron) and trapping them in magnetic minimum wells at temperatures below <strong>0.5 Kelvin</strong>.
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Experiment</th>
            <th>Location</th>
            <th>Primary Method</th>
            <th>Target Precision</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>ALPHA-g</strong></td>
            <td>CERN AD Hall</td>
            <td>Vertical magnetic trap release and annihilation vertex tracking</td>
            <td>1% of standard <em>g</em></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>AEgIS</strong></td>
            <td>CERN AD Hall</td>
            <td>Moiré deflectometer on a pulsed antihydrogen beam</td>
            <td>1% of standard <em>g</em></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>GBAR</strong></td>
            <td>CERN AD Hall</td>
            <td>Laser cooling of H̄⁺ ions followed by photodetachment free fall</td>
            <td>0.1% of standard <em>g</em></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>2. Theoretical Implications for General Relativity</h2>
      <p>
        In Einstein's General Relativity, gravity is not a traditional force but the curvature of spacetime. Any particle traveling along a geodesic follows the curvature irrespective of whether its electric charge, baryon number, or lepton number is positive or negative.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>CPT Symmetry:</strong> Combining Charge Conjugation (C), Parity (P), and Time Reversal (T) implies antimatter has positive inertial mass \(m_i > 0\).</li>
        <li><strong>Weak Equivalence Principle:</strong> \(m_g = m_i\) dictates that antimatter accelerates downward at rate \(g\).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      {faqs.map((faq) => (
        <details>
          <summary>{faq.q}</summary>
          <p>{faq.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </section>

    <section class="panel">
      <h2 style="margin-top: 0;">Explore the Antigravity Topic Cluster</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/antigravity/">Antigravity Pillar: Physics, Evidence & Experiments</a></li>
        <li><a href="/antigravity/magnetic-levitation/">Magnetic Levitation vs Antigravity</a></li>
        <li><a href="/antigravity/gravitational-shielding/">Gravitational Shielding: Podkletnov Claims Tested</a></li>
        <li><a href="/antigravity/casimir-effect/">Casimir Effect: Quantum Vacuum Forces</a></li>
        <li><a href="/antigravity/negative-mass/">Negative Mass in General Relativity</a></li>
        <li><a href="/antigravity/superconductors/">Superconductors & Flux Pinning</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
